From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: meanaverage <18336095+meanaverage@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 17:27:35 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(graphics): decode ClearCodec NSCodec regions straight
 into the image

An NSCodec region of a ClearCodec stream was decoded on its own before
it was copied into the image: each of its three or four planes whole
(raw planes copied, RLE planes expanded), then the region's BGRA pixels
into an image of their own. A region can be as large as the image, and
an RLE plane of a single run takes eleven bytes, whatever its size: a
91-byte stream of an 8192x8192 region made the decoder allocate
512 MiB, infallibly, on top of its 256 MiB image. In the WebAssembly
build, the command carrying it grew the instance every session shares
by 769 MiB, and trapped when the instance was near its 4 GiB.

The decoder now reads the planes a row at a time, decoding their runs
as it goes, and writes the region's pixels straight into the image: a
region takes a few rows, under 40 KiB for one 8192 pixels wide, and the
command grows the instance by the image's 256 MiB, or fails near 4 GiB.
It refuses what it refused: a plane larger than its raw size, an RLE
plane without its four bytes of EndData, a run missing its length, and
runs that fall short of their plane or overrun it.
